  "why": "The statement asks two things: whether infinitely many strongly regular geodetic graphs exist, and whether any non-Moore one exists. Every degree-57 Moore event (Makhnev, Faber-Keegan, Renteln, Dalfó, Mačaj-Širáň, Smith-Montemanni, Ishida) concerns only a Moore case. Settling it either way would add or remove at most one graph, so it answers neither part; I scored all of them none. Hoffman-Singleton 1960 shows that Moore graphs exist, which is not what either part asks, so it is none too. The Deutsch-Fischer and Bagchi parameter restrictions on the λ≥1 case narrow where a non-Moore example could be. They do not move a quantity that the statement asks about, so I scored them none and not bound; this was the closest call, and the outcome is the same either way because the events are pre-snapshot and would not tier as resolved. Stober-Weiß is a finite enumeration, so instances. Elder et al. is about Cayley graphs, so variant. No AI system is credited anywhere; CP-SAT is a solver.",
